    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "(To be honest, I did not choose this problem wisely. An elementary problem about trigonometry, I might have used a deeper or more complicated theorem, but I did the opposite. The answer is below, along with the derivation.)\nLet $\\triangle ABC$ be a triple angle triangle with angles $A$, $B$, and $C$. Be sure the triangle is in the second quadrant (because cosine is decreasing.)\nLet $X$, $Y$, and $P$ be points where side $BC$ remains a part of $\\triangle AXB$, $\\triangle BYC$, and $\\triangle APC$, respectively, where angle $APX=60^\\circ$.\nThen $AXB$ and $YBC$ must each have the same angles as $\\triangle XYP$. The Law of Cosines on $\\triangle XAP$ and $\\triangle YBP$ gives us\n\\[AP^2 = BC\\cdot XA = 2\\lvert\\sin 60^\\circ\\rvert \\cdot BX\\]\n$BX=BC\\cdot\\sin 60^\\circ=2PY$, since $P$ is the midpoint of $BC$.\nWe have $X \\cdot Y \\cdot BA = X\\cdot Y \\cdot XB\\cdot (BX^{\\circ}).$\nIf we let $D$ score label $BA \\cdot BC$, this means\n\\[D\\cdot 3\\times 1 \\cdot (2PY)^2=16\\pi^2\\]\n$Py + 3PX = 2\\sin 60^\\circ=\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\nSo $(1+3)(16DX^2)=16\\pi^2$, meaning\n$DX^2=\\frac{\\pi^2}{4},$\nAnd we know that $\\cos 150^\\circ = DX$.",
